{"kind":"request","text":"Add a Host-header allowlist to the resident HTTP service to defend against DNS rebinding (finding #3 of the 2026-08-11 security assessment at main 8f22ba52; re-verify at the implementation head). guardMutation (internal/service/server.go:365-378) checks Origin.Host==request.Host and Sec-Fetch-Site, which stops classic cross-origin CSRF but NOT rebinding: once a malicious page rebinds its domain to 127.0.0.1, Origin and Host both equal the attacker domain (they match) and the fetch is same-origin, so a page the operator merely visits can reach the loopback service — read /v0/status and, combined with the custodial trust model, announce a session as an existing actor (POST /v0/presence, server.go:260) and forge signed acts. Direction: reject any request whose Host is not a configured loopback host:port (derived from the bound --listen address), applied to GET reads as well as mutations; keep the existing Origin/Sec-Fetch-Site checks.","body":{"conditions":"the resident service rejects any request whose Host header is not a configured loopback host:port; the check applies to reads and mutations; the existing Origin/Sec-Fetch-Site guard is retained; a test shows a rebinding-shaped request (attacker Host) is refused; make test and make race pass; request/ branch, independent review at an exact head before merge.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123","verified_at":"main 8f22ba52; re-verify citations at the implementation head"}}